I agree.  Info on the altars, gods, effects of praying when nothing is wrong, info on messages given while offering and praying, and specifics on changing your alignment are very sparse in the Spoiler File(tm).  I haven't checked this out in the source, but here's what I have observed.

In order to convert an altar, sacrifice fresh corpses on it.  You will see a message like 'You sense a conflict between <your god> and <altar god>.'  When it tells you that you feel the power of <your god> increase, and the altar glows the color of your alignment, the altar has been converted.  You can always make an altar chaotic by sacrificing human corpses on it. This is a good way to anger your god if you are not chaotic, and a good way to please your god if you are.  It also summons demons, who will b




e friendly towards chaotics.

Changing your alignment is more complicated.  First, you have to make your god angry at you.  Then, find an altar of the alignment that you wish to convert to.  Sacrifice on it, and you will get a message along the lines of:
You feel that <your god> is angry with you
<altar god> offers you protection
You feel a sense of a new direction
and voila, new alignment.  Of course, using the altars of your old god in the future is a _bad_ idea...

As to the name of your god, it matters not...there is a set of 3 for each character class.

^L
Altars are a godsend :-) to the NetHacker.  When you find one, stay near it, and
sacrifice every corpse you get.  The tougher the monster, the more valuable the corpse.  When you see four-leaf clovers at your feet, you know your luck has gone up. Occasionally, your god will give you an object for your troubles, usually named weapons, those marvelously powerful artifacts(see the spoiler file) and eventually spellbooks. There is also the Hand of Elbereth/Servant of Arioch/ neutral equivalent? message, meaning that your god is really pleased.  If you have prayed recently, your god will be




 mildly unhappy, and sacrifices will reduce the amount of time until you can safely pray again.  You know this is happening when an offering produces the messages 'You have a hopeful feeling' and 'You have a feeling of reconciliation.' If your god is angry, offerings will produce the messages 'You have a feeling of inadequacy', 'The gods seem slightly mollified', and 'The gods seem mollified' depending on the value of the offering.  When giving all corpses to the gods, you may find yourself starving...your




 god should be happy with you at this point, so pray and your stomach will be contented.

The amount of help your god gives you when you pray, and your god's feeling towards you are directly related to your luck.  Angry gods mean negative luck, happy gods mean positive luck.  The higher your luck, the more your god will do for you when you pray.  Happy gods may save your life in any number of ways, uncurse items, bless your weapons(or anything else you wield while praying), or heal damage.  Really happy gods give you spellbooks, intrinics, or any 2 of the above.  Gods always solve problems firs




t, so solve your own problems if you want the really good prayer bonuses.

Hmm, what else?  Altars will identify the blessed/cursed status of objects dropped on them, regardless of their alignment.  Watch for amber or black flashes.  Don't identify water this way, though.  Potions of water dropped on altars change to the alignment of the altar.  Lawful altars produce holy water, chaotic altars unholy, and neutral altars ordinary.  Holy water is good for many things, see the spoiler file.

Polypiling?  Don't do it.  You don't want to know.  Stay away.  Leave it alone.
Even though you'll have all that junk lying around from those hill orcs/Mordor orcs/Uruk-Hai you just sacrificed....  

